WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.880
(fire crackling
and wind whistling)

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.240
(singing in foreign language)

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.720
(rapping in foreign language
with deep bass music)

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.560
(dramatic music)

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.720
- Let us be honest as Afrikans.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.560
Ghana does not want cocaine

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.400
so there are very
strict laws on cocaine.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.680
We've stopped it.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.960
Once you see cocaine,
somebody gets arrested.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.760
Ghana doesn't want
armed robbery.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.200
Once we see armed robbery,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.000
there is something
serious about it.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.240
As a country, we pretend
we do not want e-wastes

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.400
but we do not have laws to
stop the second-hand goods

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.960
from coming to this country.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.480
It is sheer pretense.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.680
It is sheer hypocrisy.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.200
The things we do not want
as a country, we fight it

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.000
and that is our culture.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.880
And that is it, it's about
business, about money.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.880
I mean, the second-hand
market in Ghana is very huge.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.040
This is a country where
we don't produce much,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.280
most of the things we do is

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.800
imports, trade, imports,
trade, imports, trade.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.240
Now, as a developing country,
people are beginning to get

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.080
interested in,
you know, gadgets.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.440
An unemployed country,
they don't have money

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.920
to buy brand new gadgets,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.880
so there is a huge market
for second-hand goods.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.880
(singing in foreign language)

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.040
(speaking foreign language)

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.720
(game clattering)

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.000
(speaking foreign language)

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.440
- I think as a country
it is not our priority,

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.160
we have not been
serious about it

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.640
although we know it exists

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.320
and we know it is dangerous,

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.800
there is no concerted
effort as a country

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.920
in addressing this issue.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.480
(audience applauding)

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.360
(upbeat percussion music)

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.560
(singing in foreign language)

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.160
(audience applauding)

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.560
- It is we Ghanians,
if we know this is bad

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.640
and our leadership
knows this is bad,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.120
what have they done
over the decades?

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.560
So we may cite conventions
and speak about it,

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:26.200
it's nice to put on suits
and wear a tie on TV,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:29.280
even though you know it is
bad, it is affecting Ghanians

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.880
deep down in their hearts,
in their daily activities,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.440
what are they doing to stop it?

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.840
Nothing.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.200
The leadership of this
country has, over the years,

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.920
closed one eye on
that illegality

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.840
because stopping it will
cost them some votes.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.880
- It will take Ghanians
working together

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.760
to resolve this challenge.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:56.840
- If our president
goes to sign something

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.800
to more or less assign a rating

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.800
on e-waste, even if
it's just a convention

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.960
that was signed in Geneva,
or Zuki, or Thailand,

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.640
or Europe somewhere.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.400
My grandma doesn't
know about it.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.320
She has not gone
to school before.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.280
They are in the majority.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:14.680
One day you pause this

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:16.640
and things get to
the Ghana Ports.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.360
The authorities of this country,

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.360
they charge them
tax on those goods.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.240
If you know those goods are bad,

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.320
why are you charging tax on it?

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.440
- The countries
that did the best,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.400
they had no natural resources,
no diamonds, no oil.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.880
What did they have?

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.280
Fixers.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.600
They had people in
Singapore that were buying

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:50.960
used hard-drives from the
U.S. and repairing them

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.920
until they were so good at it

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.200
that Western
Digital and Seagate,

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:56.680
they built factories there.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.120
And that's what we want
to see 10 years from now.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:04.600
- A TV repairman is
like being an engineer.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.360
That's like, they
don't have the choice

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.320
to go to MIT to become
a rocket engineer.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:14.480
They've learned to do
something themselves

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.280
by studying the textbooks.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.080
And, with their own hands,
they've fixed the wires,

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.680
fixed the circuit boards,
and make something

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.480
that somebody in
Africa can now afford.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.520
Now they can watch
the World Cup.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.280
Before, they couldn't.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:32.440
Now they can get online
and get a g-mail account.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.920
Before, they couldn't.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.680
And you know what?

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:40.640
In the end, the manufacturers
are going to be happy.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:43.160
Because the more
people in Africa

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:46.160
that can afford to
get on the internet,

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:50.560
the more people in Africa
and South America and India

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.920
that can learn to have
a Facebook account,

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:58.760
someday, those people are
going to want a new computer.

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.480
(dramatic music)

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:16.560
- The government doesn't have
the audacity, the courage,

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.680
the tenacity, to stop
some of these things.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.920
Because, you see, once
you stop it there,

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.240
it affects several
thousands of people.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:31.200
You're going to create a
huge unemployment situation.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.920
If you go to Agbogbloshie,
there are several families

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.920
who only put bread
on their table

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:41.720
through some of these things.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:43.720
They burn them,
looking for copper,

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:48.400
and they tore one aluminum
or one metal or the other.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.400
Any government that
dare say we are stopping

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:55.440
all these things that are
good is afraid to lose votes,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:58.240
is afraid to lose that
cost between voters.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:11:59.640
That is the issue.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:09.280
(speaking in foreign language)

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:39.400
- What these are,
are very young people

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:43.720
who also work here on the
downside collecting the e-waste.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:45.360
Because they are not so strong,

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.720
what they do is
to sift the soil.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.040
And they would find some
metals they also go to sell,

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:55.120
and then have some
money to buy some food.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:56.640
And then they just
weigh it there.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.640
Once they weigh their stuff,
then they take some money.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.720
Another person comes
to buy the rest,

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.080
and it is taken to the ports.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:04.560
It is taken to some companies

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:08.680
that would need copper,
or would need other metal.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:11.440
(dramatic music)

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:35.600
(speaking in foreign language)

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:12.160
(machine beeping)

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:22.120
- Out of precaution, we
found severe contamination

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:23.800
of the environment around where

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.120
the waste has been carried out,

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:26.640
with a whole range of chemicals.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.680
This is similar to
what we've found

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.760
at e-waste recycling sites in
some other countries as well.

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:35.120
So we found extremely high
levels of some toxic metals.

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:37.320
Things like lead and
cadmium, and also

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.960
a whole complex mixture
of organic chemicals.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:41.640
Now, many of these
are put in the devices

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.400
when they're
manufactured, some of them

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:47.240
are generated when the plastics
in electronics are burned.

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:49.640
A lot of the chemicals
are highly persistent.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.160
They just don't break
down, or break down

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:52.400
very slowly in the environment,

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.640
so they'll stay there
for many years to come.

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:57.040
And some of them could even
build up in the food chain.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:59.040
So this whole
mixture of chemicals

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.040
is causing severe
environmental contamination

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:03.440
around where these e-wastes
are being recycled.

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:08.080
(calming peaceful music)

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.400
- Somebody discovers
a new problem,

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:31.600
a new environmental problem,

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.400
you have to have
a solution to it.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:35.720
And that's what the Basel
Convention was for me.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.240
It was basically
the international
community's response

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.280
to the phenomenon
of waste dumping

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:44.360
from developed countries
and developing countries

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.080
where there were no controls,

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.200
people didn't even
know what it was,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:50.960
people didn't know the danger.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.200
And you had this problem.

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:54.280
You had massive amounts
of really hazardous,

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:56.160
dangerous wastes being shipped

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:57.640
from Europe and
the United States

00:17:57.640 --> 00:17:59.480
and other developed countries

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:02.600
to Africa, to Eastern Europe,
and just dumped there.

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:05.360
And people were killed,
some people died,

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.840
people got diseases, there
was massive pollution,

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:13.520
animals died, rivers and
wetlands were polluted.

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.720
So for me, it was
really, at the time,

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.680
a solution to make
people aware of this,

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:21.560
that this is really a problem,
that it had to be stopped.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:25.840
(dramatic music)

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:02.000
- My name is Ugo Vallauri

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.240
and I'm one of the co-founders
of The Restart Project,

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:09.440
which we started in
London in June, 2012.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:12.200
And we've been running
now for over two years,

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:16.320
for over 70 events
just in London alone,

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:19.440
and approximately
close to 50 events

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:21.920
outside of London
and internationally,

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:24.680
still in the past two years.

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:27.000
The Restart Project
is a social enterprise

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.720
which we created in
London with the objective

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:34.320
of changing people's
relationship with technology.

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:36.440
Learning how to repair things,

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:39.600
and by doing that, learning
more how to have control

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:44.040
over the digital devices
that are multiplying

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:46.600
in our everyday lives.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:50.400
The Restart Project was set
up with a very simple slogan

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.480
that I think is valid for
people everywhere in the world

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:57.640
and that slogan is, "Do
not despair, just repair."

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:02.320
I think, sometimes,
we underestimate

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:06.360
the powerful reality of
taking something apart,

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:11.120
learning how it works,
learning what the fault was,

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:13.400
and being personally empowered,

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:14.600
together with other individuals

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:17.560
that help you to put
it back together,

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:20.240
plug it in, and see
it working again.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:22.920
And, in a sense, our attachment

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:27.280
to that device becomes
much stronger afterwards.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:29.400
And a lot of people
end up struggling

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:31.720
with things that
they used to love

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:35.120
and that they associate
personal memories to

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:37.520
and they can't find
a way to fix them

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:39.280
so they just put
them in a corner

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.560
of their house and move on.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.960
But it is so inspiring
to see people

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:48.080
learn a new skill and learn how,

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:52.120
instead of wasting stuff,
they can give it a new life.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:21:00.600
(children singing "O
Come All Ye Faithful")

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:12.080
- The problem is not telling
us the system is dangerous.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:15.520
Every Ghanian knows the ones
who are bending these things

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:18.600
and are consuming them, it
is dangerous to my health.

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:21.200
Adults need to go
to school to know

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:23.720
that, ah, those
fumes I am inhaling

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:25.760
is dangerous to my health.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.600
Immediately, it makes
you start coughing.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.000
It's toxic, you can see it.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:30.720
You don't need to be a doctor.

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:33.800
We know it is bad, that we know.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:40.000
(radio playing
slow country music)

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:44.560
(dramatic music)

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.320
- What we did was, took
three of the leaders,

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:55.200
The Three Musketeers,
Yaro, Awal, and Razaq,

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.600
and said, hey you
guys, take four or five

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:02.640
of these piles of wires that
you were gonna burn today,

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:06.880
put them in sack,
and bring them up

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:08.680
to Tamale with us next week.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:10.440
Because we're gonna
do an experiment.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.640
We're gonna show you something.

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.880
Because it turns out that,
one of the laptop technicians

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:18.880
is from the same
village as them,

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:20.520
and his dad's a metal smith.

00:23:21.680 --> 00:23:26.240
Sule, Camodian's dad,
and Sule had these ideas

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:29.360
when we said, hey, we need
to do something constructive.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:34.360
He said, hey, what
if we cut the wire

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:36.600
in a way that it
doesn't burn it,

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:39.440
and my dad can hire some people

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:42.320
to help him make it into jewelry

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:45.800
and we can tell people,
hey, when you buy this,

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:48.760
instead of donating money
or having your taxes

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:51.480
go to a million dollar
shredding machine

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:54.120
that you can have
a direct impact?

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:57.160
And I don't know if
it's going to work,

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:02.160
but it's cool to see
an African technician

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:05.880
bring his idea to the table.

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:11.080
(dramatic music)

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:19.680
- In the north, they
don't have ways and means

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:21.480
to be able to keep
that for themselves.

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:24.680
Most of them you see here,
they don't have parents.

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:27.360
Some have single parents,
some don't at all.

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:29.480
So, due to this, they
were brought here

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:34.480
to be able to work and
send some money home,

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:36.560
and also feed themselves.

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:37.800
But when they come
here, they find out

00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:40.400
that here is not what
they have been told

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:45.200
or who people told them to be.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:49.080
So now, they are here
and they are sleeping

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:52.840
over here on this dump site,
what we call the e-waste.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.280
So they sleep here always,

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:59.440
both afternoon,
morning, and evening.

00:24:59.440 --> 00:25:01.920
They eat over here, they
do everything over here

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:03.040
by bending this scrap.

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:04.520
So when they bend this scrap,

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:05.680
they bend something small,

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:07.520
like one Ghana CD, two Ghana CD,

00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:09.560
up to, let's say, five Ghana CD.

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:11.520
Those who want to
become mechanics,

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:14.600
those who want to
become seamstress.

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.600
The boys run from there,

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:20.240
because there's no
job in the north.

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.840
So when they are here,
the things that they bend,

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:25.400
it's mostly the copper,
and then they sell it

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:26.480
to make some small money.

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:28.240
It goes in the north.

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:30.880
It is like family
and, let's say,

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:34.200
if it's not raining,
you can go to the farm.

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:36.920
(dramatic music)

